<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu Rui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 201 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu Rui was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> magistrate of Po county, popular with the Yue peoples of the south, who joined the rebellion against the Qin dynasty with his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-bu">Ying Bu</a>. In 206 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> made him King of Hengshan in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, but he supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, and after the Han victory in 202 BCE he became the first King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-rui.jpg" alt="Wu Rui" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu_Rui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wu Rui was an ethnic Yue, the son of Wu Shen, who had been grand marshal, the highest military office, of the state of Chu. As magistrate of Po County under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>, before the county was flooded, he won such standing among the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> people that he was known as the Lord of the Po.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-sheng">Chen Sheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-guang">Wu Guang</a> launched the Dazexiang Uprising against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, Wu Rui raised a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> army and joined the rebellion. His followers included Mei Xuan and his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-bu">Ying Bu</a>, both of whom later played a major part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>'s victories over the Qin and over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>. In 206 BCE Wu Rui became King of Hengshan, one of the 18 kings created under Xiang Yu, the Hegemon-King of Western Chu.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>'s victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaixia">Battle of Gaixia</a>, Wu Rui joined the other kings loyal to Liu Bang in calling on him to take the title of emperor. Soon after the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> he was moved from Hengshan to become King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wu Rui died shortly after reaching Linxiang, the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> and the capital of his new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, and was buried near the city. The kingdom passed to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-chen">Wu Chen</a>, and his descendants honoured him with the posthumous name King Wen, the civil king. His line was the only one among the kings outside the Liu family to survive beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>'s reign.</p><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> his tomb was demolished to provide wood for a new temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-jian">Sun Jian</a>. The body was so well preserved that one of those present later remarked to Wu Gang, a living descendant then serving as colonel of the Nanman, that it looked much like him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wu Rui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Wu Rui</b>, a Qin magistrate of Po county who was popular with the Yue peoples, joined the rebellion against the Qin dynasty and sent troops with his son-in-law Ying Bu.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hengshan">Kingdom of Hengshan</a></li></ul></section>
